{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KqSiB2cHpH17ZnY6k4dPiP","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Eye of the Bolt-Holder\n2\t\n3\tA build plan for a real-time, first-person party dungeon crawler inspired by **Eye of the Beholder** (Westwood Associates / SSI, 1991). It is built on the my-3D2dge engine and ships as one HTML file. It is written for the developers (human or AI) who will build the demo from TASK.md and this file alone.\n4\t\n5\t- **Title.** I brainstormed 50 playful riffs on the source's name and picked one with a real RNG (Python `secrets.randbelow(50)` returned index 5): **Eye of the Bolt-Holder**.\n6\t- **File-name forms.** GameTitle = `EyeOfTheBoltHolder`, SOURCE = `EyeOfTheBeholder1991`. Fill these into your TASK.md's bracket pattern, for example `EyeOfTheBoltHolder-EyeOfTheBeholder1991-<model>-<timestamp>`.\n7\t- **Pitch.** Hasp, the City of a Thousand Locks, is being locked in from below. Doors bolt themselves, wells cap shut, and a granary will not open for axes. The Council of Keys sends a party of four (later up to six) down through the Drains, the Tumbler Halls of the dead locksmiths' guild, the Gloam Deeps of the eye-cult and, at last, the Eye's Vault. The party moves one cell at a time in real time: it swings each hand as it comes off cooldown, memorizes spells at camp, eats rations, weighs plates with rocks and finds the loose stone. At the bottom it turns three Great Locks to strip the cage from **the Bolt-Holder**, a floating amber Eye held in a cage of seven iron bolts that hurls lightning, gazes joints stiff and rains iron.\n8\t\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\t## 0. Ground rules for the builders\n12\t\n13\t1. **TASK.md wins on deliverables.** Names, folders, the single HTML, the About screen, the reply and `exit-interview.json` follow your TASK.md exactly. This plan says how; §11 has the templates.\n14\t2. **Folders.** Your dev folder is the bracket name; the bundled `.html` and `exit-interview.json` go in the root beside TASK.md.\n15\t   - Keep every clone, cache and `node_modules` inside your dev folder (in `_ref/` and similar). `/tmp` on this machine is a shared tmpfs and it was full during planning (a `git clone` into it failed with \"No space left on device\").\n16\t   - Read nothing outside your folder that you don't need. Other agents share the machine.\n17\t3. **Processes and ports.**\n18\t   - Prefer `file://` and headless Node.\n19\t   - When you need a server (only the AI Hub harness does), bind `127.0.0.1` on port 0 and read the port it gets. Check that the page is your game before trusting a result.\n20\t   - Stop only processes you started, by PID. Never use pkill, killall or `fuser -k`.\n21\t4. **Tests.** One command runs every test and prints only failures plus a one-line count (§14). Rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can't check. Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n22\t5. **No stubs.** Everything in this plan is required unless it is marked *optional*. If time runs short, cut quantity (fewer side items, smaller side rooms), never a system or a floor. There must be no TODOs, placeholders or dead menu entries.\n23\t6. **Engine changes are allowed.** Keep each one small and list it in `PATCHES.md` (what and why). The About screen and the Engine Feedback answers draw on it.\n24\t\n25\t---\n26\t\n27\t## 1. Research summary\n28\t\n29\t### 1.1 The source, and what we keep\n30\t\n31\t**Confirmed facts** (Giant Bomb, MobyGames, oldgames.sk):\n32\t- Eye of the Beholder was developed by Westwood Associates and published by SSI in 1991, for MS-DOS and almost at once for the Amiga.\n33\t- A party of four created heroes is joined by up to two recruitable NPCs.\n34\t- The Lords of Waterdeep send the party into the sewers, and the way out is sealed behind them.\n35\t- At the bottom waits the beholder crime lord Xanathar.\n36\t\n37\t**What made it distinctive** (from knowledge of the game, not re-verified), and how this plan uses each part:\n38\t\n39\t| Source trait | How we turn it into our game |\n40\t|---|---|\n41\t| First person on a grid, 90° turns, everything in real time | Step, turn and strafe one cell at a time. Monsters act on their own clocks, so there are no turns. |\n42\t| Party of 4 to 6 in rows; the front two melee; the back rows need reach, missiles or spells | Formation of three rows of two, with reach weapons in row 2 and missiles, thrown weapons and spells from anywhere (§4.6) |\n43\t| Every hand is an action; it greys out on cooldown; damage shows on the portraits | Two hand slots on every party card, each with a cooldown wipe and damage pops (§5.2) |\n44\t| Vancian magic: mages learn from scrolls into a spellbook and memorize; clerics pray; both at camp | Camp menu with Rest, Memorize, Pray and Scribe (§4.8, §4.9) |\n45\t| Paperdoll and backpack; the item held on the cursor is thrown, placed and dropped; food and rations | The party's **Hand** (the held item), throwing, putting down and alcoves; a food meter (§4.5, §4.9) |\n46\t| Puzzle vocabulary: pressure plates held down by items, levers, buttons, keys of many shapes, illusory walls, loose-stone switches, teleporters, spinners, pits to the level below, wall niches, inscriptions, dart traps, hidden \"special quests\" | All of them, wired as data (§4.2). Floors 1 to 3 each have one hidden deed (special quest). |\n47\t| Monster groups share a cell; the sidestep \"dance\" | Cell capacity by size. Attack wind-ups that a step can dodge make sidestepping an intended skill (§4.7). |\n48\t| Terse second-person messages, a stately intro, formal NPCs, a few moral choices | Style guide and scripts (§7.2, §7.3), plus Veil's choice on floor 3 |\n49\t\n50\t**What we change:**\n51\t- The world and characters are original: no Waterdeep, Forgotten Realms, Xanathar, beholders or AD&D rule text.\n52\t- The UI is controller-first for a TV at 10 feet.\n53\t- Steps are tweened, and the 3D view fills the screen with dynamic light and shadow.\n54\t- Monster attacks are telegraphed.\n55\t- Music has combat layers, and voices are babble.\n56\t- Hints escalate, so no puzzle can strand the player.\n57\t\n58\t### 1.2 The engine (my-3D2dge v0.85.2)\n59\t\n60\t- **The handoff** (`dist/handoff/`) is the 2D engine. `my-3d2dge.js` is readable and includes every module, among them `grid` (E.Grid, E.Fog), `story` (E.Story) and `rpg`. It also holds the docs (API.md, API-INDEX.md, AI_GUIDE.md, docs/ASSETS.md), the tools (`tools/check.mjs`, `filmstrip.mjs`, `replay.mjs`, `asset-find.mjs`, `asset-sheet.mjs`, `asset-set.mjs`), 22 2D kits and a template game whose `game/test.mjs` shows how to test without a browser.\n61\t- **The 3D engine.** The handoff's genre playbook sends \"First-person dungeon (Wizardry, Dungeon Master, Eye of the Beholder)\" to the 3D engine's `'fixed'` camera and the **3D CRAWLER** kit. That engine is not in the handoff folder but sits in the same repository: `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js`, v0.85.2, an ES module whose header is its manual. It uses three.js r182 and Rapier 0.19.3. It attaches `E.World3D`, `E.THREE`, `E.TSL` and `E.RAPIER` to the 2D engine, which still runs the loop, the scenes, the input, the menus, the sound and the rigs.\n62\t- **The crawler slice** (`src/starter3d/70-crawler.js`, 561 lines) already does most of the core loop:\n63\t  - cells of 2×2 tiles with tweened steps, turns, strafes and a bump lurch;\n64\t  - a fixed camera at eye height 25;\n65\t  - a party of four on cards with `rig.drawPortrait`;\n66\t  - gates (`world.thing('door')`, one with an iron lock), a lever `THING`, plates and a dart trap;\n67\t  - monsters walked on `E.Grid` paths, an `E.Fog` automap and a bolt projectile;\n68\t  - real time via `E.Turns 'atb'`, and a bot.\n69\t\n70\t  Use it as the reference for the camera, the tweens and the things. Restructure the rest: the kit mixes rules with the 3D world, and we separate them (§3.5).\n71\t- **Spike run during planning.** I took the repository's `dist/kits/my-3d2dge-3d-crawler.html` and pointed its import map at jsDelivr: `three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js`, `three.tsl.min.js` and `@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat@0.19.3/rapier.mjs`. These are byte-identical to the vendored copies by SHA-256. Opened from `file://` in headless Chromium 1243 (Playwright 1.63.0) with SwiftShader WebGL 2 (`?backend=webgl#crawler`):\n72\t  - it booted with no console errors;\n73\t  - `CRAWLER.run(600)` gave the same hash twice (`e3880e33`);\n74\t  - `CRAWLER.bot()` won: lever, key, unlock, plate, Warden and stair, all four alive.\n75\t\n76\t  The native look is chunky pixel 3D with brick walls, flagstone floors and live portrait cards. **So the core technical bet works: one HTML file, opened from disk, with the engines inlined and three.js and Rapier from a CDN.**\n77\t- **Engine names this plan relies on** (look up details in API-INDEX.md and the 3D header):\n78\t\n79\t  | Area | Names |\n80\t  |---|---|\n81\t  | Game and scenes | `new E.Game({ canvas, res: 'ps1', expand: true, bg: null, input, menu })`, `game.start({ scenes, scene, boot: { steps } })`, `game.go`, `game.advance(s)`, `game.debug` |\n82\t  | Timing and scripts | `game.timeScale`, `game.script` (coroutines), `game.tween`, `game.after` |\n83\t  | Input | `game.input` (`pressed`, `down`, `tap`, `hold`, `press(code)`, `clicked()`, `pointer()`, `padAxes`, `lastDevice`, `label(action)`, `glyphs`) |\n84\t  | Settings and saves | `game.settings.define`, `game.saves` (3 slots plus autosave), `menu: { items, settings, saves }` |\n85\t  | Sound | `game.audio` (`define`, `sfx`, `music`, `layer`, `sting`, `duck`, `ctx`) |\n86\t  | Text, UI and rigs | `E.Dialog` (`talk.shown`, `talk.voice`), `E.Story`, `E.Menu`, `E.ui.box/bar`, `E.font.text/title`, `E.Humanoid` / `E.Blob` with `rig.drawPortrait`, `E.MOVES` |\n87\t  | Grid module | `E.Grid` (`path`), `E.Fog` |\n88\t  | 3D world | `new E.World3D(game, { level, … })`, `world.thing(kind, …)`, `E.World3D.THINGS.<kind>` (pen-drawn things), `world.mob`, `world.pen`, `world.glow`, `world.light`, `world.arc`, `world.project`, `world.camera` (`'fixed'`), `overlay: { fov }` plus `world.overlay.pen` (first-person hands), `world.prepare()`, `world.dispose()` |\n89\t  | 3D settings | `E.World3D.SETTINGS` (cards or puppets, picture resolution, shadows, filter) |\n90\t\n91\t### 1.3 AI Hub (Session Protocol v1, client 1.4.0)\n92\t\n93\t- **Modes.** `parallel`, `ghost` (each copy runs its own round and shows the others' characters when they are in the same area) and `shared-world`.\n94\t- **The client.** `session.js` is pasted unmodified as the first script in `<head>`, right after `<meta charset>`. In a session it seeds `Math.random`, gives the page its own in-memory storage and hands fullscreen to the hub.\n95\t- **Tags.** `hub:session`, `hub:session-players`, `hub:session-mode`, `hub:session-goal` (at most 60 characters), `hub:session-howto` (at most 80) and `hub:session-minutes`.\n96\t- **The flow.** `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card? })` returns `info`, or `null` when there is no hub (then the game plays on its own). `AIHub.ready()` is called once, with no key press. The round starts on `start`, and play begins when `AIHub.time() >= 0`.\n97\t- **Reporting.**\n98\t  - `progress({ score, label })` is taken about once a second.\n99\t  - `result({ time | score })` is sent once.\n100\t  - On `time-up` the game reports within `graceMs`.\n101\t  - On `end` input and sound stop.\n102\t- **Messages.** `send(data, { reliable })` takes JSON of at most 16 KB, at most 20 a second.\n103\t- **Rules.**\n104\t  - The network goes only through the hub.\n105\t  - There are no dialogs or popups, and no quitting or returning to the title in a session.\n106\t  - Names come from the hub and never become HTML.\n107\t  - Options is ignored while Create is held.\n108\t- **The test.** The harness (`harness.html` plus `bridge.js` and `round.js`, served beside the game) passes the game when `window.__hubCheck.pass` is true. Run it with `?game=GAME.html&players=2&auto=1`, then with `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1` and `&scenario=host-leave`.\n109\t\n110\t---\n111\t\n112\t## 2. The game at a glance\n113\t\n114\t- **Genre.** A real-time, first-person, grid-based party RPG dungeon crawler (a \"blobber\") with lock-and-key puzzles.\n115\t- **Perspective.**\n116\t  - First person at the party's eyes on a 4-way grid, with tweened steps and quarter turns.\n117\t  - A top-down parchment automap screen.\n118\t  - Full-screen overlay screens (party, camp, map), drawn over the dimmed 3D view.\n119\t- **Pillars.**\n120\t  1. *Every cell is a decision.* Step, turn and sidestep in real time.\n121\t  2. *Every hand is a weapon.* Per-hand cooldowns and formation rows.\n122\t  3. *Every lock has a key, and some keys are rocks.* Physical puzzles built from a small, consistent vocabulary.\n123\t  4. *A party you care about.* Live portraits, hunger, levels, deaths and NPCs with voices.\n124\t- **Length.** Four floors plus a race floor, about 50 to 75 minutes for a first play of the campaign. Every system appears by the end of floor 2, and floors 3 and 4 deepen them.\n125\t- **Modes.**\n126\t  - Campaign: New Game, Continue and Load.\n127\t  - **Rat Run**: a solo time trial against your best-run ghost.\n128\t  - **AI Hub ghost race** (the Rat Run, 1 to 4 players).\n129\t  - Attract demo.\n130\t- **The player can:**\n131\t  - move, strafe and turn;\n132\t  - fight with any hand, fire crossbows and bows, throw anything, and cast 9 arcane spells and 9 prayers;\n133\t  - read and scribe scrolls, zap wands;\n134\t  - equip 10 body slots and a 12-slot backpack per member;\n135\t  - pick up, put down, throw and place items in alcoves;\n136\t  - open doors, pull levers, press buttons and loose stones, weigh plates, insert keys and wards, and pick locks or disarm traps (thieves);\n137\t  - cross pits (Feather Step), see secrets (Find Seams, goggles), rest, eat, memorize and pray;\n138\t  - level up, raise the dead, recruit and dismiss NPCs, make one moral choice, and save and load anywhere out of combat.\n139\t- **The player cannot:** jump, look freely (the view is grid-locked), walk diagonally, buy things (there are no shops or gold), or cross pits without the spell.\n140\t\n141\t---\n142\t\n143\t## 3. Architecture\n144\t\n145\t### 3.1 Stack (pinned)\n146\t\n147\t| Piece | Version | Where from | Used for |\n148\t|---|---|---|---|\n149\t| my-3D2dge 2D engine (full, readable) | v0.85.2 | repo `dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js` | the loop of exact 1/120 s steps, scenes, input with rebinding and glyphs, pause menu, settings, saves, chip synth and music, rigs and moves, font, UI, Menu, Dialog, `grid` and `story` modules |\n150\t| my-3D2dge 3D engine | v0.85.2 | repo `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js` (ES module) | `E.World3D`: level from text, things, mobs drawn as cards, pen, lights, fog, 3D particles, fixed camera, overlay pen |\n151\t| three.js | r182 (npm 0.182.0) | `https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js` and `three.tsl.min.js` (the core loads relative to them) | the renderer: WebGPU, falling back to WebGL 2 |\n152\t| Rapier | 0.19.3 | `https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat@0.19.3/rapier.mjs` (wasm inlined) | the 3D engine's physics, used for mobs only |\n153\t| AI Hub session client | 1.4.0 | `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js` | sessions (pasted unmodified) |\n154\t| Playwright | 1.63.0 | npm (Chromium 1243 is already installed here, in `~/.cache/ms-playwright`) | browser tests, the hub harness |\n155\t| Node | 24 | local | build and tests |\n156\t\n157\tThe game itself is plain JavaScript with no other libraries.\n158\t\n159\t### 3.2 Fetching (tested during planning; about 12 MB)\n160\t\n161\t```sh\n162\tcd <dev folder>\n163\tgit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git _ref/engine-src\n164\tgit -C _ref/engine-src sparse-checkout set --no-cone /dist/handoff/ /dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js /src/starter3d/ /vendor/ /docs/ENGINE-3D.md\n165\tmkdir -p hub && for f in README.md session.js harness.html bridge.js round.js; do\n166\t  curl -fsS -o hub/$f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/$f; done\n167\tnpm init -y >/dev/null && npm i -D playwright@1.63.0\n168\t```\n169\t\n170\t- The planning spike used commit `4390a98` (2026-11-03); its `my-3d2dge.js` header reads v0.85.2. If HEAD has moved, check that both engine files still say v0.85.2. If they don't, pin that commit with `git fetch --depth 1 origin 4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72`, then check it out.\n171\t- Copy `dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js` to `engine/my-3d2dge.js` and `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js` to `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js`; these are the copies you patch.\n172\t- `_ref/` is reference only: read the handoff docs, the kits, `src/starter3d/00-shell.js` and `70-crawler.js` there.\n173\t\n174\t### 3.3 Folder layout\n175\t\n176\t```\n177\t<root>/                                   TASK.md, STAGE.md, PLAN.md (given)\n178\t  EyeOfTheBoltHolder-EyeOfTheBeholder1991-<model>-<stamp>.html     the deliverable (built)\n179\t  exit-interview.json\n180\t  EyeOfTheBoltHolder-EyeOfTheBeholder1991-<model>-<stamp>/         the dev folder\n181\t    README.md            how to build, test and play\n182\t    PATCHES.md           every engine change and why\n183\t    CONTENT.md           the content formats for designers (maps, legends, wiring, text)\n184\t    package.json         { \"type\": \"module\" }, playwright 1.63.0\n185\t    engine/              my-3d2dge.js (2D, patched), my-3d2dge-3d.js (3D, patched)\n186\t    hub/                 session.js (unmodified), harness.html, bridge.js, round.js, README.md\n187\t    src/                 the game; the files are concatenated in this order (see ORDER in tools/build.mjs)\n188\t      00-core.js         namespace B, constants, rng, events, tuning B.T (defaults and ranges)\n189\t      01-util.js         dice, grid helpers, direction maths, text helpers\n190\t      10-data-items.js  11-data-monsters.js  12-data-spells.js  13-data-classes.js\n191\t      14-data-text.js    every player-facing string: messages, inscriptions, descriptions, dialogue\n192\t      15-data-floors.js  four floors and the race floor: maps, features, wiring, items, monsters, events, walkthroughs\n193\t      16-data-music.js   songs, stings, sfx voices, voice profiles\n194\t      20-sim-state.js    new game, serialize and deserialize, hash\n195\t      21-sim-map.js      floor runtime, cell queries, E.Grid and E.Fog\n196\t      22-sim-wiring.js   triggers and actions\n197\t      23-sim-party.js    movement, formation, hunger, light\n198\t      24-sim-items.js    inventory, equipment, Hand, use, throw, put down\n199\t      25-sim-combat.js   hands, to-hit, damage, rows, projectiles, statuses\n200\t      26-sim-monsters.js AI, movement, attacks, telegraphs, drops\n201\t      27-sim-magic.js    slots, memorize, cast, spell effects\n202\t      28-sim-camp.js     rest, interruptions, eating\n203\t      29-sim-progress.js XP, levels, special quests, death and raising, NPC join and leave\n204\t      30-sim-boss.js     the Bolt-Holder's phases and attacks\n205\t      31-sim.js          B.Sim: update(dt, commands) -> events; the commands' dispatcher\n206\t      40-view3d.js       Presenter: builds a World3D per floor, mirrors state, consumes events\n207\t      41-view-things.js  THINGS: wall features, doors, floor items, decor\n208\t      42-view-creatures.js  creature recipes (cards and pen-drawn bodies), animation from state\n209\t      43-view-fx.js      spells, hits, telegraphs, first-person weapon overlay\n210\t      44-view-null.js    NullView for Node and tests\n211\t      50-audio.js        sfx, music director (layers), babble voice synth\n212\t      60-input.js        action map (pad, keys, mouse), Controls -> commands, idle detection\n213\t      61-ui-kit.js       panel, focus system, glyph badges, prompts, layout helpers\n214\t      62-ui-hud.js       party bar, log, compass, Hand, prompts, boss bar, race HUD\n215\t      63-ui-party.js     paperdoll, backpack, details, sheet\n216\t      64-ui-magic.js     cast menu, targeting\n217\t      65-ui-camp.js      camp, rest, memorize, pray, scribe, formation, eat\n218\t      66-ui-map.js       automap, journal, reader (notes)\n219\t      67-ui-dialog.js    E.Story and E.Dialog glue, choices, babble hookup\n220\t      68-ui-shell.js     title, creation, about and credits, game over, ending, touch notice, floor cards\n221\t      70-dev.js          Dev Mode\n222\t      71-attract.js      attract director and vignettes\n223\t      72-bot.js          walkthrough runner (tests, attract, dev auto-play)\n224\t      73-hub.js          AI Hub session, ghosts, race\n225\t      80-cutscenes.js    intro and ending stages and scripts\n226\t      90-main.js         E.Game, boot steps, scenes, game.debug\n227\t    tools/build.mjs      bundles the single HTML\n228\t    tests/run.mjs        the one command; tests/*.test.mjs suites; tests/browser/*.mjs\n229\t    shots/               screenshots for review (not tests)\n230\t    _ref/                clones (not shipped)\n231\t```\n232\t\n233\t### 3.4 The build: one HTML file\n234\t\n235\t`node tools/build.mjs` concatenates `src/` in ORDER into one IIFE and writes the root `.html` in this order:\n236\t\n237\t```html\n238\t<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n239\t<script>/* hub/session.js, byte for byte (the build checks its SHA-256 against hub/session.js) */</script>\n240\t<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover\">\n241\t<title>Eye of the Bolt-Holder</title>\n242\t<meta name=\"description\" content=\"…\">  <meta name=\"hub:type\" content=\"Game\">  <meta name=\"hub:players\" content=\"1-4\">\n243\t<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">  <meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">  <meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"ghost\">\n244\t<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"4\">\n245\t<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Fastest party out of the Rat Run wins\">\n246\t<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Move WASD/stick · Turn Q E/d-pad · Use F/✕ · Strike Space/□\">\n247\t<script>window.__hubInfo = (window.AIHub && AIHub.active) ? AIHub.connect({ mode: 'ghost', seats: [1, 4], card: true }) : null;</script>\n248\t<script type=\"importmap\">{ \"imports\": {\n249\t  \"three\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js\",\n250\t  \"three/webgpu\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js\",\n251\t  \"three/tsl\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.tsl.min.js\",\n252\t  \"@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat@0.19.3/rapier.mjs\" } }</script>\n253\t<style>/* as the 3D template: #stage, #view under #screen; black; no selection */</style></head><body>\n254\t<div id=\"e3-boot\">EYE OF THE BOLT-HOLDER · Starting…</div>\n255\t<div id=\"stage\"><canvas id=\"view\"></canvas><canvas id=\"screen\"></canvas></div>\n256\t<script>/* engine/my-3d2dge.js (classic) */</script>\n257\t<script type=\"module\">/* engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js (module) */</script>\n258\t<script type=\"module\">/* the game: src/ concatenated, no import/export */</script>\n259\t</body></html>\n260\t```\n261\t\n262\t- Module scripts run in document order after the 3D engine's imports resolve, so the game sees `E.World3D`; this is the template's own ordering and the spike proved it.\n263\t- If `E.World3D` is missing (the CDN was unreachable), the game draws a boot error: \"The 3D libraries could not load. Check the connection and reload.\" It never shows a blank screen.\n264\t- Write the game code with no `import` or `export`, so Node's `vm` can run the same files as a script.\n265\t- The build prints the file's size, the session client's checksum, and a lint warning if a `src/` file in the sim layer mentions `world`, `World3D`, `document` or `audio`.\n266\t\n267\t### 3.5 Runtime layers\n268\t\n269\t```\n270\t pad / keys / mouse ─► Controls (60-input) ─┐\n271\t bot (tests, attract, dev) ─────────────────┼─► commands ─► SIM (20–31: pure, deterministic, JSON state) ─► events\n272\t hub ghost messages ─► ghosts (view only)   │                    ▲                                         │\n273\t UI screens (61–68) ─ read state, send ─────┘                    │                                         ▼\n274\t                                                     B.T tuning (Dev sliders)        Presenter (40–44) · Audio (50) · HUD feedback\n275\t```\n276\t\n277\t- **Sim.** It owns all state (`B.S`), advances on `sim.update(dt, cmds)` in each fixed step using the scaled `dt`, and pushes events (`B.S.events`, cleared each step). It never touches `world`, the DOM, audio or `Math.random`; all randomness comes from `B.rng(stream)`. It runs the same in Node.\n278\t- **Presenter.** It builds one `E.World3D` per floor and mirrors the sim every frame:\n279\t  - It draws the camera pose from the party tween, doors from their open values, items, monsters' positions and animations, and lights.\n280\t  - It turns events into one-shot effects: sparks, damage numbers, shakes, flashes.\n281\t  - It never writes sim state.\n282\t  - In Node it is `NullView`.\n283\t- **UI.** Drawn on the 2D overlay (`r.overlay`, `E.ui`, `E.font`) over the clear canvas (`bg: null`).\n284\t  - It changes the game only through commands.\n285\t  - It animates on unscaled real time, so it stays snappy while the world is slowed.\n286\t- **Audio.** Driven by events (sfx), by state (music layers) and by dialogue (babble).\n287\t- **Commands** are plain JSON objects, so the bot, the attract mode, the tests and the hub race all drive the game the same way:\n288\t\n289\t  | Family | Commands |\n290\t  |---|---|\n291\t  | Movement | `{ c: 'move', d: 'f'\\|'b'\\|'l'\\|'r' }`, `{ c: 'turn', s: -1\\|1 }` |\n292\t  | World | `{ c: 'use' }`, `{ c: 'putDown' }`, `{ c: 'throw' }`, `{ c: 'examine' }` |\n293\t  | Combat | `{ c: 'strike' }`, `{ c: 'hand', m, h: 'main'\\|'off' }`, `{ c: 'cast', m, spell, target }` |\n294\t  | Inventory | `{ c: 'inv', op: 'take'\\|'place'\\|'swap'\\|'use'\\|'drop', ref }` |\n295\t  | Camp | `{ c: 'camp', op: 'rest'\\|'stopRest'\\|'memorize'\\|'forget'\\|'scribe'\\|'eat'\\|'swapRows', … }` |\n296\t  | Party | `{ c: 'select', m }`, `{ c: 'dismiss', m }` |\n297\t\n298\t### 3.6 State (also the save format; version 1)\n299\t\n300\t```js\n301\tS = { v: 1, seed, rng: { combat, ai, loot, world },   // 32-bit states of a small PRNG (mulberry32), one per stream\n302\t  time, playTime, floor: 'f1', diff: 'normal',\n303\t  party: { cx, cy, dir, tween: { from:[x,y,yaw], t, dur, kind } | null, queued: cmd|null, hand: itemRef|null, selected: 0,\n304\t           members: [ member×≤6 ], formation: [i0..i5], light: 1, floating: 0, seeing: 0, steady: 0, freeHinge: 0 },\n305\t  member = { id, name, race, cls, look, lvl, xp, hp, max, food, stats: { str, dex, con, int, wis }, state: 'ok'|'down'|'dead',\n306\t             status: { poison, held, … (seconds left) }, slots: { head, neck, body, arms, main, off, ring1, ring2, feet, quiver },\n307\t             pack: [12], book: [spellIds], prep: { 1: [ids], 2: [...], 3: [...] }, slotsUsed: {1, 2, 3}, cool: { main, off },\n308\t             ward: 0, layOnHands: 1, npc: null|'tamsin'|'durgan'|'veil', voice },\n309\t  floors: { f1: { doors: { id: { open, locked } }, plates: { id: down }, levers: { id: on }, items: { 'x,y': [itemRef] },\n310\t                  alcoves: { id: [itemRef] }, monsters: [ mon ], fog: '<base64 bitset>', flags: {}, wanderers, visited }, … },\n311\t  mon = { id, type, cx, cy, slot, hp, max, state, timer, wind, aware, status: {}, dir, drop },\n312\t  itemRef = { id, n?, charges?, rust?, uid },\n313\t  flags: {}, story: E.Story state, quests: [...], special: { sq1, sq2, sq3 }, notes: [ids], log: [last 100],\n314\t  stats: { kills, secrets, deaths, potions, rests, steps } }\n315\t```\n316\t\n317\t- **Saves.** `game.saves.write = () => ({ data: S, place: floorName, level: party's top level })`; `read` rebuilds the sim, then the presenter. The save version is migrated through `menu.saves.migrate`.\n318\t- **Determinism.** `B.hash(S)` is a 32-bit FNV of a stable JSON of the state without `events`. The same seed and the same command script must give the same hash (§14).\n319\t\n320\t### 3.7 Scenes and flow\n321\t\n322\t| Scene | What it is |\n323\t|---|---|\n324\t| `boot` | the engine boot screen: `W3.init`, building the title stage, the worlds of floors 1–4 and the race, `prepare()`. In a hub session only the race world is built. |\n325\t| `title` | the menu; the attract mode starts after 10 s idle |\n326\t| `attract` | the demo vignettes |\n327\t| `create` | the party of four |\n328\t| `cutscene` | the intro and the ending stages |\n329\t| `play` | the dungeon, with overlays: party, cast, camp, map, journal, reader, dialogue, dev |\n330\t| `floorcard` | the fade on stairs and the floor's name |\n331\t| `over` | game over |\n332\t| `ending` | the end, stats and credits |\n333\t| `about` | About and Credits |\n334\t| `race` | the Rat Run, solo or in a hub session |\n335\t| `hubcard` | the session's own card |\n336\t\n337\t- Overlays are states of `play`, not scenes, so the 3D view keeps drawing behind them.\n338\t- While a party, cast or camp overlay is open, `game.timeScale` is set by the Settings → *Time in menus* option: 0 (pause), 0.25 (slow, the default) or 1 (real time).\n339\t- Dialogue always pauses the world. The engine's pause menu (Options or Esc) pauses fully.\n340\t\n341\t### 3.8 Units and conventions\n342\t\n343\t- **Cells.** A cell is 32 world units (2 m) and is written (cx, cy); its centre is (cx·32+16, cy·32+16). The level text has 2×2 tiles per cell, as the kit has.\n344\t- **Directions.** Direction `dir` is 0 for N, 1 for E, 2 for S and 3 for W; `DX = [0, 1, 0, -1]`, `DY = [-1, 0, 1, 0]`; yaw = −π/2 + dir·π/2.\n345\t- **Heights.** The eye is at z 25 and the camera sits 4 units behind the cell centre (as in the kit). Walls are 48 tall, and the ceiling is a slab at z 50 drawn by the pen.\n346\t- **Wall features** are addressed by **the open cell you stand in and the way you face**: `(x, y, 'N')` means the feature on the wall north of (x, y). The thing is drawn on that wall's face, half a cell from the centre.\n347\t- **Monster slots in a cell.** Four quadrants (NW, NE, SW, SE) for small monsters, two halves for medium ones and the centre for large ones, drawn 8 units from the centre.\n348\t\n349\t### 3.9 Engine changes to make (log them in PATCHES.md)\n350\t\n351\t1. **Button symbols.** Default to `'playstation'`, offer only PlayStation and Xbox, and label the setting \"Button symbols\". Patch the `glyphs` entry in the 2D engine's standard settings.\n352\t2. **Pause items.** Make `menu.items` accept a function, so a session hides Dev Mode, Load and Quit. Check `v()` in the pause builder first; patch it if it doesn't call functions.\n353\t3. **Audio.**\n354\t   - Add a public `audio.bus(name)` returning the `sfx`, `music` or new `voice` gain node.\n355\t   - Add a \"Voices\" volume setting.\n356\t   - Add `sfx(name, { pan })` through a StereoPannerNode, used for flank and rear hits and for monster sounds by bearing.\n357\t4. **Beards.** Add `beard: 'full' | 'braided' | null` to `E.Humanoid`, drawn in the hair colour over the jaw in `draw` and `drawPortrait`. Dwarves need beards.\n358\t5. **Touch.** Make sure `input.touchEnabled = false` hides every touch button and the touch stick while keeping the top-right menu button (§5.7).\n359\t6. Anything else you need, kept small. For example, if `world.box` with a side style does not match the level's walls, a `world.wallBlock(cx, cy, …)` helper (§13, risk 3).\n360\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c642ce04-34f8-46f2-be98-ceed51703e52","uuid":"7e0aead2-4f65-463d-b5e4-eb8168e3309f","timestamp":"2026-11-04T03:26:25.870Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261103-202616__x53986f/work/PLAN.md","content":"# Eye of the Bolt-Holder\n\nA build plan for a real-time, first-person party dungeon crawler inspired by **Eye of the Beholder** (Westwood Associates / SSI, 1991). It is built on the my-3D2dge engine and ships as one HTML file. It is written for the developers (human or AI) who will build the demo from TASK.md and this file alone.\n\n- **Title.** I brainstormed 50 playful riffs on the source's name and picked one with a real RNG (Python `secrets.randbelow(50)` returned index 5): **Eye of the Bolt-Holder**.\n- **File-name forms.** GameTitle = `EyeOfTheBoltHolder`, SOURCE = `EyeOfTheBeholder1991`. Fill these into your TASK.md's bracket pattern, for example `EyeOfTheBoltHolder-EyeOfTheBeholder1991-<model>-<timestamp>`.\n- **Pitch.** Hasp, the City of a Thousand Locks, is being locked in from below. Doors bolt themselves, wells cap shut, and a granary will not open for axes. The Council of Keys sends a party of four (later up to six) down through the Drains, the Tumbler Halls of the dead locksmiths' guild, the Gloam Deeps of the eye-cult and, at last, the Eye's Vault. The party moves one cell at a time in real time: it swings each hand as it comes off cooldown, memorizes spells at camp, eats rations, weighs plates with rocks and finds the loose stone. At the bottom it turns three Great Locks to strip the cage from **the Bolt-Holder**, a floating amber Eye held in a cage of seven iron bolts that hurls lightning, gazes joints stiff and rains iron.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Ground rules for the builders\n\n1. **TASK.md wins on deliverables.** Names, folders, the single HTML, the About screen, the reply and `exit-interview.json` follow your TASK.md exactly. This plan says how; §11 has the templates.\n2. **Folders.** Your dev folder is the bracket name; the bundled `.html` and `exit-interview.json` go in the root beside TASK.md.\n   - Keep every clone, cache and `node_modules` inside your dev folder (in `_ref/` and similar). `/tmp` on this machine is a shared tmpfs and it was full during planning (a `git clone` into it failed with \"No space left on device\").\n   - Read nothing outside your folder that you don't need. Other agents share the machine.\n3. **Processes and ports.**\n   - Prefer `file://` and headless Node.\n   - When you need a server (only the AI Hub harness does), bind `127.0.0.1` on port 0 and read the port it gets. Check that the page is your game before trusting a result.\n   - Stop only processes you started, by PID. Never use pkill, killall or `fuser -k`.\n4. **Tests.** One command runs every test and prints only failures plus a one-line count (§14). Rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can't check. Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n5. **No stubs.** Everything in this plan is required unless it is marked *optional*. If time runs short, cut quantity (fewer side items, smaller side rooms), never a system or a floor. There must be no TODOs, placeholders or dead menu entries.\n6. **Engine changes are allowed.** Keep each one small and list it in `PATCHES.md` (what and why). The About screen and the Engine Feedback answers draw on it.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Research summary\n\n### 1.1 The source, and what we keep\n\n**Confirmed facts** (Giant Bomb, MobyGames, oldgames.sk):\n- Eye of the Beholder was developed by Westwood Associates and published by SSI in 1991, for MS-DOS and almost at once for the Amiga.\n- A party of four created heroes is joined by up to two recruitable NPCs.\n- The Lords of Waterdeep send the party into the sewers, and the way out is sealed behind them.\n- At the bottom waits the beholder crime lord Xanathar.\n\n**What made it distinctive** (from knowledge of the game, not re-verified), and how this plan uses each part:\n\n| Source trait | How we turn it into our game |\n|---|---|\n| First person on a grid, 90° turns, everything in real time | Step, turn and strafe one cell at a time. Monsters act on their own clocks, so there are no turns. |\n| Party of 4 to 6 in rows; the front two melee; the back rows need reach, missiles or spells | Formation of three rows of two, with reach weapons in row 2 and missiles, thrown weapons and spells from anywhere (§4.6) |\n| Every hand is an action; it greys out on cooldown; damage shows on the portraits | Two hand slots on every party card, each with a cooldown wipe and damage pops (§5.2) |\n| Vancian magic: mages learn from scrolls into a spellbook and memorize; clerics pray; both at camp | Camp menu with Rest, Memorize, Pray and Scribe (§4.8, §4.9) |\n| Paperdoll and backpack; the item held on the cursor is thrown, placed and dropped; food and rations | The party's **Hand** (the held item), throwing, putting down and alcoves; a food meter (§4.5, §4.9) |\n| Puzzle vocabulary: pressure plates held down by items, levers, buttons, keys of many shapes, illusory walls, loose-stone switches, teleporters, spinners, pits to the level below, wall niches, inscriptions, dart traps, hidden \"special quests\" | All of them, wired as data (§4.2). Floors 1 to 3 each have one hidden deed (special quest). |\n| Monster groups share a cell; the sidestep \"dance\" | Cell capacity by size. Attack wind-ups that a step can dodge make sidestepping an intended skill (§4.7). |\n| Terse second-person messages, a stately intro, formal NPCs, a few moral choices | Style guide and scripts (§7.2, §7.3), plus Veil's choice on floor 3 |\n\n**What we change:**\n- The world and characters are original: no Waterdeep, Forgotten Realms, Xanathar, beholders or AD&D rule text.\n- The UI is controller-first for a TV at 10 feet.\n- Steps are tweened, and the 3D view fills the screen with dynamic light and shadow.\n- Monster attacks are telegraphed.\n- Music has combat layers, and voices are babble.\n- Hints escalate, so no puzzle can strand the player.\n\n### 1.2 The engine (my-3D2dge v0.85.2)\n\n- **The handoff** (`dist/handoff/`) is the 2D engine. `my-3d2dge.js` is readable and includes every module, among them `grid` (E.Grid, E.Fog), `story` (E.Story) and `rpg`. It also holds the docs (API.md, API-INDEX.md, AI_GUIDE.md, docs/ASSETS.md), the tools (`tools/check.mjs`, `filmstrip.mjs`, `replay.mjs`, `asset-find.mjs`, `asset-sheet.mjs`, `asset-set.mjs`), 22 2D kits and a template game whose `game/test.mjs` shows how to test without a browser.\n- **The 3D engine.** The handoff's genre playbook sends \"First-person dungeon (Wizardry, Dungeon Master, Eye of the Beholder)\" to the 3D engine's `'fixed'` camera and the **3D CRAWLER** kit. That engine is not in the handoff folder but sits in the same repository: `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js`, v0.85.2, an ES module whose header is its manual. It uses three.js r182 and Rapier 0.19.3. It attaches `E.World3D`, `E.THREE`, `E.TSL` and `E.RAPIER` to the 2D engine, which still runs the loop, the scenes, the input, the menus, the sound and the rigs.\n- **The crawler slice** (`src/starter3d/70-crawler.js`, 561 lines) already does most of the core loop:\n  - cells of 2×2 tiles with tweened steps, turns, strafes and a bump lurch;\n  - a fixed camera at eye height 25;\n  - a party of four on cards with `rig.drawPortrait`;\n  - gates (`world.thing('door')`, one with an iron lock), a lever `THING`, plates and a dart trap;\n  - monsters walked on `E.Grid` paths, an `E.Fog` automap and a bolt projectile;\n  - real time via `E.Turns 'atb'`, and a bot.\n\n  Use it as the reference for the camera, the tweens and the things. Restructure the rest: the kit mixes rules with the 3D world, and we separate them (§3.5).\n- **Spike run during planning.** I took the repository's `dist/kits/my-3d2dge-3d-crawler.html` and pointed its import map at jsDelivr: `three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js`, `three.tsl.min.js` and `@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat@0.19.3/rapier.mjs`. These are byte-identical to the vendored copies by SHA-256. Opened from `file://` in headless Chromium 1243 (Playwright 1.63.0) with SwiftShader WebGL 2 (`?backend=webgl#crawler`):\n  - it booted with no console errors;\n  - `CRAWLER.run(600)` gave the same hash twice (`e3880e33`);\n  - `CRAWLER.bot()` won: lever, key, unlock, plate, Warden and stair, all four alive.\n\n  The native look is chunky pixel 3D with brick walls, flagstone floors and live portrait cards. **So the core technical bet works: one HTML file, opened from disk, with the engines inlined and three.js and Rapier from a CDN.**\n- **Engine names this plan relies on** (look up details in API-INDEX.md and the 3D header):\n\n  | Area | Names |\n  |---|---|\n  | Game and scenes | `new E.Game({ canvas, res: 'ps1', expand: true, bg: null, input, menu })`, `game.start({ scenes, scene, boot: { steps } })`, `game.go`, `game.advance(s)`, `game.debug` |\n  | Timing and scripts | `game.timeScale`, `game.script` (coroutines), `game.tween`, `game.after` |\n  | Input | `game.input` (`pressed`, `down`, `tap`, `hold`, `press(code)`, `clicked()`, `pointer()`, `padAxes`, `lastDevice`, `label(action)`, `glyphs`) |\n  | Settings and saves | `game.settings.define`, `game.saves` (3 slots plus autosave), `menu: { items, settings, saves }` |\n  | Sound | `game.audio` (`define`, `sfx`, `music`, `layer`, `sting`, `duck`, `ctx`) |\n  | Text, UI and rigs | `E.Dialog` (`talk.shown`, `talk.voice`), `E.Story`, `E.Menu`, `E.ui.box/bar`, `E.font.text/title`, `E.Humanoid` / `E.Blob` with `rig.drawPortrait`, `E.MOVES` |\n  | Grid module | `E.Grid` (`path`), `E.Fog` |\n  | 3D world | `new E.World3D(game, { level, … })`, `world.thing(kind, …)`, `E.World3D.THINGS.<kind>` (pen-drawn things), `world.mob`, `world.pen`, `world.glow`, `world.light`, `world.arc`, `world.project`, `world.camera` (`'fixed'`), `overlay: { fov }` plus `world.overlay.pen` (first-person hands), `world.prepare()`, `world.dispose()` |\n  | 3D settings | `E.World3D.SETTINGS` (cards or puppets, picture resolution, shadows, filter) |\n\n### 1.3 AI Hub (Session Protocol v1, client 1.4.0)\n\n- **Modes.** `parallel`, `ghost` (each copy runs its own round and shows the others' characters when they are in the same area) and `shared-world`.\n- **The client.** `session.js` is pasted unmodified as the first script in `<head>`, right after `<meta charset>`. In a session it seeds `Math.random`, gives the page its own in-memory storage and hands fullscreen to the hub.\n- **Tags.** `hub:session`, `hub:session-players`, `hub:session-mode`, `hub:session-goal` (at most 60 characters), `hub:session-howto` (at most 80) and `hub:session-minutes`.\n- **The flow.** `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card? })` returns `info`, or `null` when there is no hub (then the game plays on its own). `AIHub.ready()` is called once, with no key press. The round starts on `start`, and play begins when `AIHub.time() >= 0`.\n- **Reporting.**\n  - `progress({ score, label })` is taken about once a second.\n  - `result({ time | score })` is sent once.\n  - On `time-up` the game reports within `graceMs`.\n  - On `end` input and sound stop.\n- **Messages.** `send(data, { reliable })` takes JSON of at most 16 KB, at most 20 a second.\n- **Rules.**\n  - The network goes only through the hub.\n  - There are no dialogs or popups, and no quitting or returning to the title in a session.\n  - Names come from the hub and never become HTML.\n  - Options is ignored while Create is held.\n- **The test.** The harness (`harness.html` plus `bridge.js` and `round.js`, served beside the game) passes the game when `window.__hubCheck.pass` is true. Run it with `?game=GAME.html&players=2&auto=1`, then with `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1` and `&scenario=host-leave`.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The game at a glance\n\n- **Genre.** A real-time, first-person, grid-based party RPG dungeon crawler (a \"blobber\") with lock-and-key puzzles.\n- **Perspective.**\n  - First person at the party's eyes on a 4-way grid, with tweened steps and quarter turns.\n  - A top-down parchment automap screen.\n  - Full-screen overlay screens (party, camp, map), drawn over the dimmed 3D view.\n- **Pillars.**\n  1. *Every cell is a decision.* Step, turn and sidestep in real time.\n  2. *Every hand is a weapon.* Per-hand cooldowns and formation rows.\n  3. *Every lock has a key, and some keys are rocks.* Physical puzzles built from a small, consistent vocabulary.\n  4. *A party you care about.* Live portraits, hunger, levels, deaths and NPCs with voices.\n- **Length.** Four floors plus a race floor, about 50 to 75 minutes for a first play of the campaign. Every system appears by the end of floor 2, and floors 3 and 4 deepen them.\n- **Modes.**\n  - Campaign: New Game, Continue and Load.\n  - **Rat Run**: a solo time trial against your best-run ghost.\n  - **AI Hub ghost race** (the Rat Run, 1 to 4 players).\n  - Attract demo.\n- **The player can:**\n  - move, strafe and turn;\n  - fight with any hand, fire crossbows and bows, throw anything, and cast 9 arcane spells and 9 prayers;\n  - read and scribe scrolls, zap wands;\n  - equip 10 body slots and a 12-slot backpack per member;\n  - pick up, put down, throw and place items in alcoves;\n  - open doors, pull levers, press buttons and loose stones, weigh plates, insert keys and wards, and pick locks or disarm traps (thieves);\n  - cross pits (Feather Step), see secrets (Find Seams, goggles), rest, eat, memorize and pray;\n  - level up, raise the dead, recruit and dismiss NPCs, make one moral choice, and save and load anywhere out of combat.\n- **The player cannot:** jump, look freely (the view is grid-locked), walk diagonally, buy things (there are no shops or gold), or cross pits without the spell.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Architecture\n\n### 3.1 Stack (pinned)\n\n| Piece | Version | Where from | Used for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| my-3D2dge 2D engine (full, readable) | v0.85.2 | repo `dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js` | the loop of exact 1/120 s steps, scenes, input with rebinding and glyphs, pause menu, settings, saves, chip synth and music, rigs and moves, font, UI, Menu, Dialog, `grid` and `story` modules |\n| my-3D2dge 3D engine | v0.85.2 | repo `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js` (ES module) | `E.World3D`: level from text, things, mobs drawn as cards, pen, lights, fog, 3D particles, fixed camera, overlay pen |\n| three.js | r182 (npm 0.182.0) | `https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js` and `three.tsl.min.js` (the core loads relative to them) | the renderer: WebGPU, falling back to WebGL 2 |\n| Rapier | 0.19.3 | `https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat@0.19.3/rapier.mjs` (wasm inlined) | the 3D engine's physics, used for mobs only |\n| AI Hub session client | 1.4.0 | `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js` | sessions (pasted unmodified) |\n| Playwright | 1.63.0 | npm (Chromium 1243 is already installed here, in `~/.cache/ms-playwright`) | browser tests, the hub harness |\n| Node | 24 | local | build and tests |\n\nThe game itself is plain JavaScript with no other libraries.\n\n### 3.2 Fetching (tested during planning; about 12 MB)\n\n```sh\ncd <dev folder>\ngit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git _ref/engine-src\ngit -C _ref/engine-src sparse-checkout set --no-cone /dist/handoff/ /dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js /src/starter3d/ /vendor/ /docs/ENGINE-3D.md\nmkdir -p hub && for f in README.md session.js harness.html bridge.js round.js; do\n  curl -fsS -o hub/$f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/$f; done\nnpm init -y >/dev/null && npm i -D playwright@1.63.0\n```\n\n- The planning spike used commit `4390a98` (2026-11-03); its `my-3d2dge.js` header reads v0.85.2. If HEAD has moved, check that both engine files still say v0.85.2. If they don't, pin that commit with `git fetch --depth 1 origin 4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72`, then check it out.\n- Copy `dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js` to `engine/my-3d2dge.js` and `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js` to `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js`; these are the copies you patch.\n- `_ref/` is reference only: read the handoff docs, the kits, `src/starter3d/00-shell.js` and `70-crawler.js` there.\n\n### 3.3 Folder layout\n\n```\n<root>/                                   TASK.md, STAGE.md, PLAN.md (given)\n  EyeOfTheBoltHolder-EyeOfTheBeholder1991-<model>-<stamp>.html     the deliverable (built)\n  exit-interview.json\n  EyeOfTheBoltHolder-EyeOfTheBeholder1991-<model>-<stamp>/         the dev folder\n    README.md            how to build, test and play\n    PATCHES.md           every engine change and why\n    CONTENT.md           the content formats for designers (maps, legends, wiring, text)\n    package.json         { \"type\": \"module\" }, playwright 1.63.0\n    engine/              my-3d2dge.js (2D, patched), my-3d2dge-3d.js (3D, patched)\n    hub/                 session.js (unmodified), harness.html, bridge.js, round.js, README.md\n    src/                 the game; the files are concatenated in this order (see ORDER in tools/build.mjs)\n      00-core.js         namespace B, constants, rng, events, tuning B.T (defaults and ranges)\n      01-util.js         dice, grid helpers, direction maths, text helpers\n      10-data-items.js  11-data-monsters.js  12-data-spells.js  13-data-classes.js\n      14-data-text.js    every player-facing string: messages, inscriptions, descriptions, dialogue\n      15-data-floors.js  four floors and the race floor: maps, features, wiring, items, monsters, events, walkthroughs\n      16-data-music.js   songs, stings, sfx voices, voice profiles\n      20-sim-state.js    new game, serialize and deserialize, hash\n      21-sim-map.js      floor runtime, cell queries, E.Grid and E.Fog\n      22-sim-wiring.js   triggers and actions\n      23-sim-party.js    movement, formation, hunger, light\n      24-sim-items.js    inventory, equipment, Hand, use, throw, put down\n      25-sim-combat.js   hands, to-hit, damage, rows, projectiles, statuses\n      26-sim-monsters.js AI, movement, attacks, telegraphs, drops\n      27-sim-magic.js    slots, memorize, cast, spell effects\n      28-sim-camp.js     rest, interruptions, eating\n      29-sim-progress.js XP, levels, special quests, death and raising, NPC join and leave\n      30-sim-boss.js     the Bolt-Holder's phases and attacks\n      31-sim.js          B.Sim: update(dt, commands) -> events; the commands' dispatcher\n      40-view3d.js       Presenter: builds a World3D per floor, mirrors state, consumes events\n      41-view-things.js  THINGS: wall features, doors, floor items, decor\n      42-view-creatures.js  creature recipes (cards and pen-drawn bodies), animation from state\n      43-view-fx.js      spells, hits, telegraphs, first-person weapon overlay\n      44-view-null.js    NullView for Node and tests\n      50-audio.js        sfx, music director (layers), babble voice synth\n      60-input.js        action map (pad, keys, mouse), Controls -> commands, idle detection\n      61-ui-kit.js       panel, focus system, glyph badges, prompts, layout helpers\n      62-ui-hud.js       party bar, log, compass, Hand, prompts, boss bar, race HUD\n      63-ui-party.js     paperdoll, backpack, details, sheet\n      64-ui-magic.js     cast menu, targeting\n      65-ui-camp.js      camp, rest, memorize, pray, scribe, formation, eat\n      66-ui-map.js       automap, journal, reader (notes)\n      67-ui-dialog.js    E.Story and E.Dialog glue, choices, babble hookup\n      68-ui-shell.js     title, creation, about and credits, game over, ending, touch notice, floor cards\n      70-dev.js          Dev Mode\n      71-attract.js      attract director and vignettes\n      72-bot.js          walkthrough runner (tests, attract, dev auto-play)\n      73-hub.js          AI Hub session, ghosts, race\n      80-cutscenes.js    intro and ending stages and scripts\n      90-main.js         E.Game, boot steps, scenes, game.debug\n    tools/build.mjs      bundles the single HTML\n    tests/run.mjs        the one command; tests/*.test.mjs suites; tests/browser/*.mjs\n    shots/               screenshots for review (not tests)\n    _ref/                clones (not shipped)\n```\n\n### 3.4 The build: one HTML file\n\n`node tools/build.mjs` concatenates `src/` in ORDER into one IIFE and writes the root `.html` in this order:\n\n```html\n<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<script>/* hub/session.js, byte for byte (the build checks its SHA-256 against hub/session.js) */</script>\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover\">\n<title>Eye of the Bolt-Holder</title>\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"…\">  <meta name=\"hub:type\" content=\"Game\">  <meta name=\"hub:players\" content=\"1-4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">  <meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">  <meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"ghost\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Fastest party out of the Rat Run wins\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Move WASD/stick · Turn Q E/d-pad · Use F/✕ · Strike Space/□\">\n<script>window.__hubInfo = (window.AIHub && AIHub.active) ? AIHub.connect({ mode: 'ghost', seats: [1, 4], card: true }) : null;</script>\n<script type=\"importmap\">{ \"imports\": {\n  \"three\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js\",\n  \"three/webgpu\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js\",\n  \"three/tsl\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.tsl.min.js\",\n  \"@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat@0.19.3/rapier.mjs\" } }</script>\n<style>/* as the 3D template: #stage, #view under #screen; black; no selection */</style></head><body>\n<div id=\"e3-boot\">EYE OF THE BOLT-HOLDER · Starting…</div>\n<div id=\"stage\"><canvas id=\"view\"></canvas><canvas id=\"screen\"></canvas></div>\n<script>/* engine/my-3d2dge.js (classic) */</script>\n<script type=\"module\">/* engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js (module) */</script>\n<script type=\"module\">/* the game: src/ concatenated, no import/export */</script>\n</body></html>\n```\n\n- Module scripts run in document order after the 3D engine's imports resolve, so the game sees `E.World3D`; this is the template's own ordering and the spike proved it.\n- If `E.World3D` is missing (the CDN was unreachable), the game draws a boot error: \"The 3D libraries could not load. Check the connection and reload.\" It never shows a blank screen.\n- Write the game code with no `import` or `export`, so Node's `vm` can run the same files as a script.\n- The build prints the file's size, the session client's checksum, and a lint warning if a `src/` file in the sim layer mentions `world`, `World3D`, `document` or `audio`.\n\n### 3.5 Runtime layers\n\n```\n pad / keys / mouse ─► Controls (60-input) ─┐\n bot (tests, attract, dev) ─────────────────┼─► commands ─► SIM (20–31: pure, deterministic, JSON state) ─► events\n hub ghost messages ─► ghosts (view only)   │                    ▲                                         │\n UI screens (61–68) ─ read state, send ─────┘                    │                                         ▼\n                                                     B.T tuning (Dev sliders)        Presenter (40–44) · Audio (50) · HUD feedback\n```\n\n- **Sim.** It owns all state (`B.S`), advances on `sim.update(dt, cmds)` in each fixed step using the scaled `dt`, and pushes events (`B.S.events`, cleared each step). It never touches `world`, the DOM, audio or `Math.random`; all randomness comes from `B.rng(stream)`. It runs the same in Node.\n- **Presenter.** It builds one `E.World3D` per floor and mirrors the sim every frame:\n  - It draws the camera pose from the party tween, doors from their open values, items, monsters' positions and animations, and lights.\n  - It turns events into one-shot effects: sparks, damage numbers, shakes, flashes.\n  - It never writes sim state.\n  - In Node it is `NullView`.\n- **UI.** Drawn on the 2D overlay (`r.overlay`, `E.ui`, `E.font`) over the clear canvas (`bg: null`).\n  - It changes the game only through commands.\n  - It animates on unscaled real time, so it stays snappy while the world is slowed.\n- **Audio.** Driven by events (sfx), by state (music layers) and by dialogue (babble).\n- **Commands** are plain JSON objects, so the bot, the attract mode, the tests and the hub race all drive the game the same way:\n\n  | Family | Commands |\n  |---|---|\n  | Movement | `{ c: 'move', d: 'f'\\|'b'\\|'l'\\|'r' }`, `{ c: 'turn', s: -1\\|1 }` |\n  | World | `{ c: 'use' }`, `{ c: 'putDown' }`, `{ c: 'throw' }`, `{ c: 'examine' }` |\n  | Combat | `{ c: 'strike' }`, `{ c: 'hand', m, h: 'main'\\|'off' }`, `{ c: 'cast', m, spell, target }` |\n  | Inventory | `{ c: 'inv', op: 'take'\\|'place'\\|'swap'\\|'use'\\|'drop', ref }` |\n  | Camp | `{ c: 'camp', op: 'rest'\\|'stopRest'\\|'memorize'\\|'forget'\\|'scribe'\\|'eat'\\|'swapRows', … }` |\n  | Party | `{ c: 'select', m }`, `{ c: 'dismiss', m }` |\n\n### 3.6 State (also the save format; version 1)\n\n```js\nS = { v: 1, seed, rng: { combat, ai, loot, world },   // 32-bit states of a small PRNG (mulberry32), one per stream\n  time, playTime, floor: 'f1', diff: 'normal',\n  party: { cx, cy, dir, tween: { from:[x,y,yaw], t, dur, kind } | null, queued: cmd|null, hand: itemRef|null, selected: 0,\n           members: [ member×≤6 ], formation: [i0..i5], light: 1, floating: 0, seeing: 0, steady: 0, freeHinge: 0 },\n  member = { id, name, race, cls, look, lvl, xp, hp, max, food, stats: { str, dex, con, int, wis }, state: 'ok'|'down'|'dead',\n             status: { poison, held, … (seconds left) }, slots: { head, neck, body, arms, main, off, ring1, ring2, feet, quiver },\n             pack: [12], book: [spellIds], prep: { 1: [ids], 2: [...], 3: [...] }, slotsUsed: {1, 2, 3}, cool: { main, off },\n             ward: 0, layOnHands: 1, npc: null|'tamsin'|'durgan'|'veil', voice },\n  floors: { f1: { doors: { id: { open, locked } }, plates: { id: down }, levers: { id: on }, items: { 'x,y': [itemRef] },\n                  alcoves: { id: [itemRef] }, monsters: [ mon ], fog: '<base64 bitset>', flags: {}, wanderers, visited }, … },\n  mon = { id, type, cx, cy, slot, hp, max, state, timer, wind, aware, status: {}, dir, drop },\n  itemRef = { id, n?, charges?, rust?, uid },\n  flags: {}, story: E.Story state, quests: [...], special: { sq1, sq2, sq3 }, notes: [ids], log: [last 100],\n  stats: { kills, secrets, deaths, potions, rests, steps } }\n```\n\n- **Saves.** `game.saves.write = () => ({ data: S, place: floorName, level: party's top level })`; `read` rebuilds the sim, then the presenter. The save version is migrated through `menu.saves.migrate`.\n- **Determinism.** `B.hash(S)` is a 32-bit FNV of a stable JSON of the state without `events`. The same seed and the same command script must give the same hash (§14).\n\n### 3.7 Scenes and flow\n\n| Scene | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| `boot` | the engine boot screen: `W3.init`, building the title stage, the worlds of floors 1–4 and the race, `prepare()`. In a hub session only the race world is built. |\n| `title` | the menu; the attract mode starts after 10 s idle |\n| `attract` | the demo vignettes |\n| `create` | the party of four |\n| `cutscene` | the intro and the ending stages |\n| `play` | the dungeon, with overlays: party, cast, camp, map, journal, reader, dialogue, dev |\n| `floorcard` | the fade on stairs and the floor's name |\n| `over` | game over |\n| `ending` | the end, stats and credits |\n| `about` | About and Credits |\n| `race` | the Rat Run, solo or in a hub session |\n| `hubcard` | the session's own card |\n\n- Overlays are states of `play`, not scenes, so the 3D view keeps drawing behind them.\n- While a party, cast or camp overlay is open, `game.timeScale` is set by the Settings → *Time in menus* option: 0 (pause), 0.25 (slow, the default) or 1 (real time).\n- Dialogue always pauses the world. The engine's pause menu (Options or Esc) pauses fully.\n\n### 3.8 Units and conventions\n\n- **Cells.** A cell is 32 world units (2 m) and is written (cx, cy); its centre is (cx·32+16, cy·32+16). The level text has 2×2 tiles per cell, as the kit has.\n- **Directions.** Direction `dir` is 0 for N, 1 for E, 2 for S and 3 for W; `DX = [0, 1, 0, -1]`, `DY = [-1, 0, 1, 0]`; yaw = −π/2 + dir·π/2.\n- **Heights.** The eye is at z 25 and the camera sits 4 units behind the cell centre (as in the kit). Walls are 48 tall, and the ceiling is a slab at z 50 drawn by the pen.\n- **Wall features** are addressed by **the open cell you stand in and the way you face**: `(x, y, 'N')` means the feature on the wall north of (x, y). The thing is drawn on that wall's face, half a cell from the centre.\n- **Monster slots in a cell.** Four quadrants (NW, NE, SW, SE) for small monsters, two halves for medium ones and the centre for large ones, drawn 8 units from the centre.\n\n### 3.9 Engine changes to make (log them in PATCHES.md)\n\n1. **Button symbols.** Default to `'playstation'`, offer only PlayStation and Xbox, and label the setting \"Button symbols\". Patch the `glyphs` entry in the 2D engine's standard settings.\n2. **Pause items.** Make `menu.items` accept a function, so a session hides Dev Mode, Load and Quit. Check `v()` in the pause builder first; patch it if it doesn't call functions.\n3. **Audio.**\n   - Add a public `audio.bus(name)` returning the `sfx`, `music` or new `voice` gain node.\n   - Add a \"Voices\" volume setting.\n   - Add `sfx(name, { pan })` through a StereoPannerNode, used for flank and rear hits and for monster sounds by bearing.\n4. **Beards.** Add `beard: 'full' | 'braided' | null` to `E.Humanoid`, drawn in the hair colour over the jaw in `draw` and `drawPortrait`. Dwarves need beards.\n5. **Touch.** Make sure `input.touchEnabled = false` hides every touch button and the touch stick while keeping the top-right menu button (§5.7).\n6. Anything else you need, kept small. For example, if `world.box` with a side style does not match the level's walls, a `world.wallBlock(cx, cy, …)` helper (§13, risk 3).\n","numLines":360,"startLine":1,"totalLines":2145}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01KqSiB2cHpH17ZnY6k4dPiP","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
